BCC_Q({"y": 2023, "n": 8, "part": "B", "topic": "graph", "title": "Restoring Music", "answer": 1, "story": "<p>Juno has found an ancient diagram that describes how to compose\npieces of music using just five types of notes. Any note can be selected\nas the first note, but a note can only be selected next if there is an\narrow pointing to it from the previous note.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:40.0%\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc3')\" style=\"box-shadow: none;\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Restoring Music<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc3\" style=\"display: none;\">\n<p>Five circles, each with a different image inside representing a type\nof note. These notes will be numbered 1 through 5. Arrows point from one\nnote to another as follows:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is an arrow pointing from 1 to 2.<\/li>\n<li>There are arrows pointing from 2 to 1 and from 2 to 3.<\/li>\n<li>There are arrows pointing from 3 to 1 and from 3 to 2.<\/li>\n<li>There are arrows pointing from 4 to 1 and from 4 to 5.<\/li>\n<li>There are arrows pointing from 5 to 3 and from 5 to 4.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/><\/div>\n<p>Juno has also found the sheet containing a piece of music which was\ncomposed using the diagram above. Two notes are missing due to a hole\ntorn in the sheet as shown below.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p> <img alt=\"A row of ten notes with two of them missing. From left to right, the notes are 5, 4, 5, 3, 1, 2, missing note, missing note, 2, 3.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:40.0%\"/> <\/p>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>Juno would like to restore this sheet of music. Which two notes must\nbe missing?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li><img alt=\"Note 5\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> <img alt=\"Note 4\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/><\/li>\n<li><img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/><\/li>\n<li><img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> <img alt=\"Note 2\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/><\/li>\n<li><img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> <img alt=\"Note 2\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/><\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(B) <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/><\/p>", "explanation": "<p>There are two arrows pointing <em>away<\/em> from the note <img alt=\"number 2\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> which means there are two possibilities for the first missing note: <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> or <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/>.<\/p>\n<p>There are two arrows pointing <em>to<\/em> the note <img alt=\"number 2\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> which means there are two possibilities for the second missing note: <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> or <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/>.<\/p>\n<p>Since there is no arrow from <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> to itself, and no arrow from <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> to <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/>, the first missing note cannot be <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> and it must be  \n<img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/>.<\/p>\n<p>Since there is no arrow from <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> to itself, but there\n<em>is<\/em> an arrow from <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> to <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/>, the second missing note must be <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/>.<\/p>\n<p>Therefore, the two missing notes are <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/>.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>The diagram describing how to compose music is an example of a\n<em>directed graph<\/em>. Each note is represented as a <em>vertex<\/em>\nof the directed graph. Each <em>(directed) edge<\/em> will go from one\nnote to the next possible note.<\/p>\n<p>The reason we say the graph is \"directed\" is because the order of the\nnotes is only valid in one direction. For example, there is an edge\nfrom <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/> to <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/>, but not the other way around. We would call <img alt=\"Note 1\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> a directed <em>neighbour<\/em> of <img alt=\"Note 3\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1cm\"/>.<\/p>\n<p>In this task, we want to find a <em>directed path<\/em> between the\ntwo <img alt=\"number 2\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> notes. One way to find this path is to perform a <em>breadth-first search<\/em>, where all of the neighbours of the <img alt=\"number 2\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> note are determined, and then repeatedly determine the neighbours of each of those neighbours, until the second <img alt=\"number 2\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"height:1.3cm\"/> note is found.<\/p>\n<p>The idea of searching for a path in a directed graph has many\napplications, such as mapping out a driving route, determining how to\nsend information through the internet, and determining recommendations\nfor who you may want to connect with on social media platforms.<\/p>", "mc": 1});